## AI Usage

Claude was used as a structured teaching and development partner throughout this project. Rather than simply providing finished code, Claude walked through each concept step by step — waiting for my confirmation before moving to the next step — and created practice exercises at key points to test my understanding before we built the actual script.

Specifically, Claude:

- Taught the ETL pattern (Extract, Transform, Load) and how it applies to threat intelligence aggregation
- Explained normalization and the `.get()` chaining technique used to translate vendor field names
- Created practice exercises where I had to correctly map normalized indicators to firewall and SIEM output formats — confirming my understanding of how the same data gets shaped differently for different consumers
- Explained deduplication using Python dictionary keys as a cleaner alternative to nested loops
- Clarified the normalize/denormalize concept — translating vendor schemas into a standard internal format at the start, then translating back out to each consumer's format at the end
- Generated the three vendor JSON test files with appropriate duplicates and validation errors
- Wrote comments throughout the script using higher education analogies drawn from my own work in transfer credit evaluation and student onboarding at ATCC

All code was reviewed and explained before inclusion. I was comfortable and familiar with all concepts covered in this assignment.

---

## Challenges

The concepts in this assignment — normalization, deduplication, filtering, and multi-format output — were all familiar and manageable. The most valuable part of the process was working through practice exercises that required me to correctly transform a normalized indicator into both firewall and SIEM formats before seeing the answers. This confirmed that I understood not just what the code does, but why each field maps the way it does for each consumer.